{"id":"h288","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"A chemist has one mole of X atoms. He finds that when half of the X atoms transfer one electron to the other half, 409 kJ must be added. If all of the resulting X^- ions are subsequently converted to X+ ions, an additional 733 kJ must be added. Find the ionization potential, and the electron affinity of X.\nIs the correct answer \"Ionization potential: 11.84eV/atom, Electron affinity: 3.36eV/atom\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"Transferring an electron from half the atoms to the other half costs 0.5(IE − EA) per mole of X, so IE − EA = 818 kJ/mol.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Converting the 0.5 mol of X⁻ to X⁺ costs (IE + EA) per ion, so 0.5(IE + EA) = 733 kJ and IE + EA = 1466 kJ/mol.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Adding the two equations gives IE = 1142 kJ/mol, which is about 11.84 eV.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Subtracting the equations gives EA = 324 kJ/mol, which is about 3.36 eV.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The sign convention is consistent, since removing an electron from X⁻ absorbs an energy equal to EA.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The bookkeeping could go wrong by mishandling the factor of 0.5 or the sign of EA, but on checking the algebra holds.","confidence":0.2,"lean":-1,"theta":0.3142,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The stated answer of 11.84 eV for ionization potential and 3.36 eV for electron affinity is correct.","confidence":0.92,"lean":1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.6,"relation":"supp